  "slug": "ancient-plants-0668",
  "summary": "Recent preprints and papers apply spatial, kinetic and editing-aware statistics to mouse brain, lung and inflammatory transcriptomes.",
  "synthesis": "The items share a statistical emphasis on encoding biological structure within the model rather than treating expression as flat counts. Spatial NT-seq estimates RNA turnover kinetics in mouse brain, with machine learning linking kinetics to mRNA stability features and regulators. A repeat-aware pipeline quantifies A-to-I editing in mouse SINE B2 RNAs, identifying hotspots that rise early in amyloid beta pathology. The inflammatome derives a 2,000-gene set and a 100-gene inflammation signature with a sample-wise score across inflammatory diseases. SM3DD retains 3D tissue geometry through segmentation-free transcript-to-transcript distances in lung tissue. The items are heterogeneous and report no mutual benchmarks. What is new is the explicit incorporation of kinetic, editing, cross-disease and spatial structure into the analytic representation itself.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-01-31"
}
